From 65f28fe467966c4f1268bf09ee64651057345297 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Keith Packard <keithp@keithp.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 03:55:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 02/12] pwd, grp: Search the databases a line at a time, and
 report only real errors

The lookups read each line into the caller's buffer, so:

 - a line that didn't fit gave ERANGE even when it wasn't the entry
   asked for, and a missing entry with a small buffer was ERANGE, not
   "not found";
 - a malformed line (or, through strsep, one with an empty last field
   such as an empty shell) ended the search with EINVAL;
 - getgrnam_r and friends could write the member array's terminator
   past the end of the buffer (a negative ptrdiff_t divided by
   sizeof);
 - the gid field wasn't checked, ids were parsed with strtol (so
   4294967294 became LONG_MAX on 32-bit targets) and an empty uid read
   as 0;
 - getpwnam, getpwuid, getgrnam, getgrgid, getpwent and getgrent set
   errno to ENOENT when nothing matched or at the end of the database,
   where POSIX says errno shall not change.

Now each line is read with getline and parsed in place, lines that
aren't entries are skipped, and only the entry found is copied into
the caller's buffer, with ERANGE (and, for a seekable stream, the
position kept so the entry can be read again) when it doesn't fit.
The files are opened close-on-exec.
